<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Borgund Stave Church</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1200</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Borgund Stave Church is a wooden church in Lærdal in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, built around 1180–1250 and dedicated to the apostle Andrew. With its tiered roofs, dragon-head gables and carved portals it is the best-preserved of Norway's medieval stave churches and the model for many later copies. It ceased to be a parish church in 1868 and is now a museum.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/borgund-stave-church.jpg" alt="Borgund Stave Church" width="320" height="496" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ximonic (Simo Räsänen) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Borgund_Stave_Church_in_L%C3%A6rdalen,_2013_June.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Borgund Stave Church was the village church of Borgund and belonged to Lærdal parish, in the Diocese of Bjørgvin. It is classified as a triple-nave stave church of the Sogn type. Its walls are vertical boards, or staves, tongued and grooved to interlock, with four corner posts joined by ground sills on a stone foundation. The plan is basilican with reduced side aisles, and a chancel and apse were added in the 14th century. Structurally it has been described as a cube within a cube, with twelve free-standing columns carrying the raised central nave, and Saint Andrew's crosses joining them above the arcade.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The church has tiered, overhanging roofs with a shingled steeple, and a stylised dragon's head on each of its four gables, which resemble the carved dragon heads on Norse ship prows. The present heads may be 18th-century replacements. Their function is uncertain and disputed, and they may have been meant to ward off evil spirits. The west portal is carved with dragons biting each other, and the three entrances are narrow, supposedly to keep evil spirits out along with the churchgoers. They were originally painted green, red, black and white.</p><p>The interior is dark. Most internal fittings have been removed, leaving a soapstone font, a medieval stone altar, an altarpiece of 1654 and a pulpit from 1550–1570. Runic inscriptions survive, among them the common Ave Maria and a note by Þórir blaming the pagan Norns for his troubles. A local story says that deer antlers on the pillars are all that remains of a reindeer shot when it tried to enter during Mass. To the south stands <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>'s only surviving free-standing stave-built bell tower, probably built in the mid-13th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In 1868 a new Borgund Church was built nearby and the old one was turned into a museum, which saved it from the demolition common among stave churches then. The Society for the Preservation of Ancient Norwegian Monuments bought it in 1877, after a restoration in the early 1870s led by Christian Christie, who aimed to return it to its pre-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> condition. The first English guidebook appeared in 1898.</p><p>It served as the example for the reconstruction of Fantoft Stave Church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a> in 1883 and its rebuilding in 1997, and the Gustav Adolf Stave Church at Hahnenklee in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, built in 1908, is modelled on it. Four replicas exist in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Borgund Stave Church” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Church, organised under the archbishopric of Nidaros (Trondheim) from 1152–1153, built stone cathedrals and stave churches such as Urnes and <b>Borgund</b> and became the largest landowner.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
